presence_conference Module
==========================

Overview
--------

The presence_conference module implements the SIP conference event package
(RFC 4575): it answers SUBSCRIBE requests for "Event: conference" and sends
NOTIFY bodies of type application/conference-info+xml describing the live
participant roster of a conference.

It is designed to run on the IMS MMTel-AS, which acts as the conference focus
(RFC 4579 section 4.4 / 3GPP TS 24.147). The actual media mixing is performed by
a FreeSWITCH MRF (MRFP); this module sources the roster from the mixer over the
FreeSWITCH Event Socket Library (ESL) using the `freeswitch` module's API.

In-dialog subscriptions
-----------------------

VoLTE UEs subscribe to the conference state *in-dialog*, reusing the focus
INVITE dialog (RFC 4575 section 3 / RFC 5057 multiple dialog usages): the
SUBSCRIBE carries the To-tag the MRF minted when it answered the conference
INVITE, and loose-routes back through the MMTel-AS record-route. The OpenSIPS
presence engine only serves subscriptions on dialogs it created itself, so it
cannot adopt such a foreign-owned dialog (it answers 481). This module therefore
implements the notifier directly on top of the dialog the MMTel-AS already
tracks for the conference (created by the cfg conf_create route):

  * conference_subscribe() accepts the in-dialog SUBSCRIBE (200 OK) and emits
    the initial / refresh conference-info+xml NOTIFY on the focus dialog. The
    dialog module's send_indialog_request() supplies the route set, target and
    CSeq from the stored dialog; the per-NOTIFY monotonic "version" attribute is
    patched in.
  * conference_notify() rebuilds the roster and fans a fresh NOTIFY out to every
    watcher of the conference when the mixer membership changes. It is driven by
    the FreeSWITCH conference::maintenance event (wired in the OpenSIPS script
    through the freeswitch_scripting module's E_FREESWITCH event_route).

Watcher state is kept in a small in-memory (shared) registry keyed by conference
id; each entry references the tracked focus dialog and is removed when that
dialog is destroyed (BYE / timeout). No database is used.

Dependencies
------------

  * dialog (in-dialog NOTIFY delivery, dialog tracking)
  * tm (SUBSCRIBE 200 OK)
  * freeswitch (ESL roster)
  * libxml2 (build dependency)

Parameters
----------

  mrf_esl_url (string)
      FreeSWITCH ESL URL of the MRF, used to run "conference xml_list".
      Format: fs://[[user]:password@]host[:port]
      Example: "fs://:ClueCon@my-mrf:8021"
      If unset, rosters are served empty.

      The module lists all mixer conferences and matches the one whose name
      equals the focus URI conference id (comparing the part before any '@'),
      so it is robust to FreeSWITCH naming the conference "<id>",
      "<id>@default" or "<id>@<domain>".

  conf_user_prefix (string, default "conf=")
      User-part prefix that identifies a conference focus URI
      (sip:conf=<id>@conf-factory...). The conference id is the user part with
      this prefix stripped.

  default_expires (integer, default 3600)
      Default subscription expiry for the conference event.

Functions
---------

  conference_subscribe(presentity)
      Accept an in-dialog conference event-package SUBSCRIBE on the focus dialog
      and emit the initial / refresh roster NOTIFY. `presentity` is the
      conference focus URI (the SUBSCRIBE Request-URI, $ru). The cfg must run
      loose_route() and t_newtran() before calling it (t_newtran absorbs UDP
      retransmissions). On Expires: 0 it sends a terminating NOTIFY and drops
      the watcher. Usable only from the request route.

  conference_notify(presentity)
      Rebuild the roster for the given focus presentity URI from the MRF mixer
      and send a NOTIFY to every active watcher. Intended to be called from
      event_route[E_FREESWITCH] on conference::maintenance events.

Privacy
-------

Participants whose caller identity is restricted (CLIR, TS 24.147 / TS 24.607)
are anonymized in the roster (entity sip:anonymous@anonymous.invalid, no
display name). Detection is heuristic, based on the caller-id rendered by
FreeSWITCH.
